WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.769
Let's get started by first looking at the concepts

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.771
underlying multi-vector retrieval.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:08.174
To help you get oriented, let's start by seeing

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.677
how it works for text. Let's dive in.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:12.879
I assume if you are taking this course,

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:16.483
you are already somewhat familiar with the fundamentals of vector retrieval

00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:20.186
and concepts like dense embedding vectors, vector similarity,

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:23.189
approximate nearest neighbor search algorithms,

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:26.192
and the HNSW indexes that power them.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:29.195
If not, I highly recommend you take Qdrant's first course

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:31.197
here with DeepLearning,

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:34.200
which introduces you to all those concepts.

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:38.004
Either way, let's do a quick review of what retrieval is

00:00:38.405 --> 00:00:41.808
and how it works. Retrieval is important because LLMs

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:44.110
can't possibly be trained on all information

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.079
relevant to the prompts they will receive.

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:48.648
Retrieval Augmented Generation, or RAG,

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:51.618
pairs an LLM with a database of relevant information.

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:55.555
When the system receives a prompt, it will quickly search the database

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.957
and retrieve the most relevant documents.

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:01.061
The original prompt is then modified to include the

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:03.396
retrieve documents, allowing the LLM

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:06.599
to consider that information while generating its response.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.369
RAG can significantly improve the quality

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:11.304
of LLM responses

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:14.307
and is one of the most widely implemented types

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:15.909
of generative AI systems.

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:18.912
For example, a customer service chatbot

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:22.115
would be much more helpful if it can access your company's

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:24.117
product and policy information.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.119
Companies achieve that

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:28.321
by pairing an LLM with data sources

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.924
from which relevant information can be retrieved.

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:35.128
The most common type of vector retrieval is called a Bi-encoder.

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.764
Every document in the knowledge base is

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.400
assigned a vector representation by an embedding model.

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:43.837
When a query is received, it is also embedded to a vector.

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:47.140
The system will then search the database to find the documents

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.510
whose vectors are located closest to the query vector.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.514
The primary benefit of Bi-encoders is that they are very fast.

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:56.816
One reason for this is that the

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:59.052
document vectors can be precomputed ahead of time.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:02.622
Even if you have a billion documents in your database,

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.958
you only need to generate their vectors once.

00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:07.026
At search time, you are only

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:09.028
generating a single vector for the query.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:11.998
The search process itself is also extremely fast.

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:16.002
Thanks to the use of approximate nearest neighbors algorithms,

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.805
they easily scale to billions of documents.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.308
The main issue with the Bi-encoder is that they essentially

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.144
compress the meaning of each document

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.312
and prompt down to a single vector.

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:30.950
This can leave out the meaning of the documents and prompts

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.553
and can miss nuanced connections between them.

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:35.855
Cross-encoders take a different approach.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:39.559
The full text of the query and document are fed

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:42.061
to the cross-encoder neural network at the same time.

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:45.265
This lets the cross-encoder detect nuanced relationships

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:47.767
or interactions in meaning

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:50.270
between the query and document.

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:53.506
The Cross-encoder will then directly output a relevance score

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:56.009
which can be used to rank documents.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:03:01.314
Cross-encoders tend to produce very high quality results, but they scale horribly.

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:05.318
Since they require the full document and query text to generate scores,

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:09.022
no pre-computation is possible, and every document you score

00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:13.526
requires a computationally expensive forward pass through the neural network.

00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:17.730
As a result, Cross-encoders are only feasible to use when searching

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:21.034
numbers of documents, usually 100 or fewer.

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.536
The best use of a cross-encoder is

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:25.872
to rerank the results retrieved by other techniques.

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:29.542
Let's say you had 1 million documents to search through.

00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:31.744
You can use a bi-encoder to

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:34.013
quickly find the 50 best matching documents,

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:36.916
and then rerank those results with a cross-encoder

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.485
to find the five absolute best matches.

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:44.824
Ideally, you'd have a technique that enables pre-computation like a bi-encoder

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:47.760
and deep token level interactions between the

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.530
query and documents like with a cross-encoder.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.033
Multi-vector techniques were introduced to do just that.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.236
So let's have a look at how they work.

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:59.505
The most common multi-vector technique for text is called ColBERT.

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:02.175
Let's see how it works with a simple example.

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:04.444
Let's say this is the text of your document.

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:07.347
First of all, the text is split into tokens.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.884
Next, these tokens are each assigned their own embedding vector.

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:16.189
These vectors not only reflect the meaning of that particular token,

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:19.559
but also its meaning in the context of the whole document.

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:22.128
If this were a bi-encoder,

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:24.731
the next step would be to reduce all these vectors

00:04:24.731 --> 00:04:27.133
down to a single vector through a process called pooling.

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:30.937
The most common pooling approach is to just average all these vectors,

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:33.940
giving a single vector representation of the whole document.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.810
In ColBERT, however, you keep all of these vectors.

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:40.913
The process of representing a query works identically.

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:43.416
The query text is converted to tokens

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:46.419
and each token is assigned a dense semantic vector.

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:49.789
Now, let's score this query document pair.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.493
The approach is called maximum similarity or simply MaxSim.

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:56.296
The idea is that each query token

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:58.298
finds the most similar document token.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:01.834
This is what it look like for a token puppy.

00:05:02.335 --> 00:05:04.304
The vector for puppy is compared

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:06.639
to the vectors of each document token

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.941
using a typical vector distance measure

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:11.244
like the dot product or cosine distance.

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:13.279
Here you would expect it to

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.214
match most closely with the token dog.

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:18.618
This process is then repeated for every token in the query.

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:21.721
The MaxSim score of this query document pair

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:25.124
is then just the sum of the maximum similarities

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:27.226
over all the query tokens.

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:32.298
MaxSim is different from other distance measures in that it is asymmetrical.

00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:35.101
The distance between A and B is not

00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:37.537
the same as the distance between B and A.

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:40.640
To convince yourself of that, imagine you just flipped

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.775
the text used for the document and the

00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:44.911
query in the last example as shown here.

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:49.115
Originally, the MaxSim was the sum of three dot products,

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:51.184
but now it's a sum of five.

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:54.620
As a result, you can't take advantage of the highly optimized

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.923
approximate nearest neighbors algorithms

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:59.192
that typically power vector search.

00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:02.962
The issue arises when you try to build the HNSW index

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:05.331
that the algorithm uses for search.

00:06:05.732 --> 00:06:08.234
This graph relies of knowing the distances

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:11.637
between each document and a few of its nearest neighbors.

00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:13.940
Since MaxSim is asymmetric,

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.442
A might be a nearest neighbor of B,

00:06:16.743 --> 00:06:20.246
but B isn't necessarily a nearest neighbor of A.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:23.983
This inconsistency prevents building the graph at all.

00:06:24.283 --> 00:06:26.686
And so when you're using multi-vector search,

00:06:26.886 --> 00:06:29.188
the HNSW index is usually disabled.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.891
ColBERT ultimately delivers on its goals.

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:36.028
It allows precomputation of vectors like a bi-encoder,

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:40.733
while the late interactions between the query and document tokens emulate cross-encoders.

00:06:41.134 --> 00:06:45.037
Since HNSW cannot be used effectively for multi-vector retrieval,

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:48.841
you rarely use them alone for search over bigger data sets.

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:50.910
If you did, then you would need to perform

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.713
a brute force full scan search over all the documents,

00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:56.215
which is too slow to use at scale.

00:06:56.416 --> 00:06:58.351
A more common way of using

00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:00.620
late interaction models is as a re-ranker

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:03.423
of the results of faster techniques like a bi-encoder.

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:07.660
Typically, a faster retriever is used with oversampling,

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:11.664
meaning you retrieve more documents than you eventually want to return.

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:14.367
You then use the multi-vectors as a reranking step.

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:18.337
So the MaxSim is only calculated on a limited set of candidates.

00:07:18.838 --> 00:07:22.942
The biggest downside of ColBERT is the amount of memory it requires.

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:25.745
With one vector stored per token,

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:28.047
you can easily have hundreds or

00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:30.750
thousands of vectors stored for every document.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:35.655
These numbers add up quickly, especially if you have a million-scale setup,

00:07:35.655 --> 00:07:37.757
which isn't that uncommon nowadays.

00:07:37.924 --> 00:07:40.126
Imagine your document has a thousand tokens.

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:43.830
Each of them gets a 128 dimensional embedding,

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:47.433
and each number is represented by a 4 byte float.

00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:50.403
That's half a megabyte just for the vectors.

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:53.806
A bi-encoder only stores a single vector per document.

00:07:54.207 --> 00:07:56.809
Even if your vectors are very high dimensional,

00:07:57.109 --> 00:07:59.412
you usually won't exceed even 10 kilobytes.

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:03.282
for document, which makes the scale easier to manage.

00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:05.685
Late interaction models are a middle ground

00:08:05.685 --> 00:08:08.187
between single vector embedding models and cross-encoders.

00:08:08.287 --> 00:08:10.690
They need more memory and are more expensive to run

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.792
than any other embedding models,

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:16.295
yet they still allow to precompute the representations of the documents

00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:18.698
and do not require to pass them through

00:08:18.698 --> 00:08:20.700
the neural network for each query.

00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:23.336
More recently, we started to see much value

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:26.539
in applying similar methods to other modalities than text.

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:29.742
And they also seem to be working better than their predecessors.

00:08:30.042 --> 00:08:32.845
Before we discuss different modalities, let's explore

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.947
how late interaction models are used in practice.

00:08:35.448 --> 00:08:37.650
We'll start by loading ColBERT V2,

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:40.219
a late interaction model developed at Stanford.

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:43.222
ColBERT encodes texts into a 128

00:08:43.222 --> 00:08:45.224
dimensional vectors for each token,

00:08:45.324 --> 00:08:47.426
enabling fine-grained semantic matching.

00:08:47.727 --> 00:08:49.629
Let's load the model into memory.

00:08:49.862 --> 00:08:52.565
and see its embedding dimensionality.

00:08:53.065 --> 00:08:55.067
It's 128.

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:58.871
Before we create embeddings, let's understand how ColBERT processes text.

00:08:59.372 --> 00:09:01.374
Tokenization is a process of breaking text

00:09:01.374 --> 00:09:03.643
into smaller pieces that the model can process.

00:09:04.043 --> 00:09:06.546
ColBERT uses WordPiece tokenization and adds

00:09:06.546 --> 00:09:09.448
special tokens like CLS at the start,

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:11.951
unused one for document encoding,

00:09:12.051 --> 00:09:13.653
and SEP at the end.

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:17.256
Regular words are either represented as separate tokens

00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:19.892
or sometimes a word may be split

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:22.528
into multiple tokens too. Let's define our document.

00:09:22.862 --> 00:09:26.532
We have a simple helper method to handle the tokenization process.

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:29.035
If you are interested in the technical details,

00:09:29.035 --> 00:09:32.538
feel free to check the function definition in the helper.py file.

00:09:32.972 --> 00:09:36.442
Let's see how our sample document gets tokenized.

00:09:37.944 --> 00:09:41.847
Majority of words are represented as single tokens.

00:09:42.548 --> 00:09:46.452
However, decibels is split into three separate tokens

00:09:46.452 --> 00:09:48.554
which are on a subword level.

00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:53.159
Let's simply check how many tokens our document was divided into.

00:09:53.526 --> 00:09:56.128
Our document consists of 55 tokens.

00:09:56.829 --> 00:10:00.866
Now that you understand tokenization, let's create embeddings for your document.

00:10:01.334 --> 00:10:05.204
Unlike traditional dense models that produce a single vector,

00:10:05.204 --> 00:10:07.840
ColBERT generates one embedding vector for each token.

00:10:08.107 --> 00:10:11.143
And as you saw, our document has 55 tokens,

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:16.549
so ColBERT should also create 55 separate 128-dimensional vectors.

00:10:16.916 --> 00:10:20.019
One for each token in the tokenized sequence.

00:10:20.519 --> 00:10:24.824
Notice the shape. This confirms we have 55 token embeddings,

00:10:24.824 --> 00:10:27.526
each represented by a 128 dimensional vector.

00:10:27.827 --> 00:10:31.330
In practice, when working with longer document or data chunks,

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:33.432
you can expect hundreds of tokens per embedding.

00:10:33.432 --> 00:10:35.334
But the principle remains the same.

00:10:35.434 --> 00:10:37.136
One vector per token.

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:40.473
Now let's define and encode your query,

00:10:41.073 --> 00:10:43.476
which is way shorter than the document.

00:10:43.876 --> 00:10:46.946
ColBERT encodes queries differently than documents.

00:10:47.346 --> 00:10:50.850
It pads queries to a fixed length of 32 tokens

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.152
to ensure consistent comparison across.

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:57.390
different queries, but it also assumes a query cannot be any longer.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:00.192
While your query has only a few words,

00:11:00.192 --> 00:11:02.695
ColBERT will generate 32 token embeddings

00:11:02.695 --> 00:11:05.398
with extra padding tokens to reach this length.

00:11:05.698 --> 00:11:08.300
If you make it longer, it may get cut off.

00:11:08.801 --> 00:11:13.406
As expected, we got 32, 128 dimensional embeddings,

00:11:13.406 --> 00:11:15.408
even though our query was relatively short.

00:11:15.641 --> 00:11:18.611
Let's see how the tokenization of the query looks like

00:11:18.611 --> 00:11:21.313
using the same helper method like we used for the document.

00:11:21.814 --> 00:11:24.617
It has an additional parameter to control

00:11:24.617 --> 00:11:28.421
whether we create tokenization for the document or a query.

00:11:28.721 --> 00:11:31.323
As you can see, majority of the tokens

00:11:31.323 --> 00:11:33.325
are just some padding tokens

00:11:33.325 --> 00:11:36.028
which are automatically added to the query.

00:11:36.328 --> 00:11:38.831
Nevertheless, each of the words of the original

00:11:38.831 --> 00:11:41.300
query was just converted into a single token.

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:44.637
Now, let's see late interaction in action.

00:11:45.237 --> 00:11:47.306
We'll compute the similarity matrix

00:11:47.406 --> 00:11:49.108
by taking a dot product

00:11:49.108 --> 00:11:51.310
between every query token embedding

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:53.713
and every document token embedding.

00:11:53.979 --> 00:11:57.616
This creates a 32 by 55 matrix

00:11:57.983 --> 00:12:00.152
where each cell represents a similarity

00:12:00.453 --> 00:12:03.956
between a specific query token and a specific document token.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:07.393
In the next step, you will only select the maximum similarity

00:12:07.393 --> 00:12:09.595
for each of the query tokens,

00:12:09.595 --> 00:12:12.598
and sum them up across all of them.

00:12:13.099 --> 00:12:17.603
maxsim_score of our document for the query is around 17.

00:12:18.037 --> 00:12:21.140
Let's visualize the similarity matrix as a heat map.

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:24.944
We have another helper function that does exactly this.

00:12:25.544 --> 00:12:28.848
Each cell shows how similar a specific query token

00:12:28.948 --> 00:12:31.550
is to a specific document token.

00:12:31.951 --> 00:12:34.153
Lighter colors indicate lower similarity

00:12:34.153 --> 00:12:37.857
and the red border is used to highlight the highest score.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:42.795
You can see which query terms like advantages or cars

00:12:42.795 --> 00:12:45.798
match most strongly with which document terms

00:12:45.798 --> 00:12:47.900
like benefits and fleets.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.302
This visualization helps you to understand

00:12:50.302 --> 00:12:53.806
why ColBERT finds certain token pairs relevant.

00:12:54.073 --> 00:12:57.176
For clarity, all the mask tokens are removed from the query,

00:12:57.176 --> 00:12:59.678
but they also contribute to the final score.

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:02.681
To appreciate the benefits of late interaction,

00:13:02.681 --> 00:13:05.718
let's compare ColBERT with a traditional dense embedding model.

00:13:05.718 --> 00:13:08.721
You will load one of the popular embedding models from fastembed

00:13:09.221 --> 00:13:11.223
and use it alongside ColBERT

00:13:11.223 --> 00:13:13.526
to see how their search results differ in practice.

00:13:13.926 --> 00:13:15.928
Now you will create a Qdrant collection

00:13:15.928 --> 00:13:18.531
that can store both dense and multi-vector embeddings.

00:13:18.831 --> 00:13:22.234
You will keep the collection name and vector names as variables.

00:13:22.601 --> 00:13:25.571
So you can easily refer to them later on.

00:13:25.771 --> 00:13:27.773
Now the real collection setup.

00:13:27.873 --> 00:13:30.075
Notice the special configuration for ColBERT.

00:13:30.276 --> 00:13:33.679
You enable multivector config with the MaxSim comparator,

00:13:33.779 --> 00:13:37.783
which tells Qdrant to use the MaxSim scoring that we discussed earlier.

00:13:38.017 --> 00:13:40.820
You also disable HNSW graph creation

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:43.222
by setting the m parameter to zero.

00:13:43.823 --> 00:13:47.026
Since this data structure cannot be effectively used for multi-vector search.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.262
Let's create some example documents

00:13:50.663 --> 00:13:52.965
about Qdrant and SQL databases

00:13:53.165 --> 00:13:55.367
to test the search functionality.

00:13:55.968 --> 00:13:58.070
Now you will insert these documents

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:00.673
into the collection you created earlier.

00:14:01.073 --> 00:14:03.275
you will create both dense embedding

00:14:03.275 --> 00:14:05.878
and multi-vector representation

00:14:05.878 --> 00:14:07.580
using the ColBERT model.

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:10.082
Now, let's search using both approaches.

00:14:10.583 --> 00:14:12.451
We'll create a helper function.

00:14:12.451 --> 00:14:15.654
that will query the collection using ColBERT embeddings.

00:14:16.255 --> 00:14:18.457
We pass the query and the vector is generated

00:14:18.457 --> 00:14:20.659
using the model you defined earlier.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:23.262
Please mind that you are calling query_embed,

00:14:23.262 --> 00:14:24.964
not passage_embed anymore,

00:14:25.264 --> 00:14:29.635
because certain models may process queries differently than the documents.

00:14:29.935 --> 00:14:33.339
You can also specify the number of results you want to retrieve.

00:14:33.839 --> 00:14:36.442
Another helpful function will perform the same process

00:14:36.742 --> 00:14:39.144
but using the regular dense embedding model.

00:14:39.645 --> 00:14:42.748
You have both methods defined, so let's create a query.

00:14:43.115 --> 00:14:46.418
Feel free to play with different queries to see different outputs.

00:14:46.418 --> 00:14:50.122
You might be wondering why we specifically selected this particular query.

00:14:50.422 --> 00:14:52.725
Proper names, such as the names of companies,

00:14:52.925 --> 00:14:55.027
are quite often divided into multiple tokens,

00:14:55.227 --> 00:14:57.429
consisting of even single letters.

00:14:57.730 --> 00:15:00.232
Such a token cannot effectively represent

00:15:00.332 --> 00:15:02.434
what that particular company does,

00:15:02.635 --> 00:15:04.236
and it's more like a fallback.

00:15:04.436 --> 00:15:06.171
Even though we all know Qdrant is

00:15:06.171 --> 00:15:07.940
a vector search engine at this point,

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:11.944
for our ColBERT model, it's nothing more than a sequence of letters.

00:15:12.444 --> 00:15:16.615
To confirm that our query is divided into that many tokens,

00:15:16.615 --> 00:15:20.219
let's use the same helper function like we used before.

00:15:20.719 --> 00:15:23.422
Indeed Qdrant was divided into three separate tokens,

00:15:23.422 --> 00:15:26.425
what proves that ColBERT was not exposed to

00:15:26.926 --> 00:15:29.728
the name of the company in this training data.

00:15:29.962 --> 00:15:31.797
In dense embedding models, this kind of

00:15:31.797 --> 00:15:33.866
tokens will contribute to the final pooled vector,

00:15:35.067 --> 00:15:38.771
but they will be most likely just some kind of a noise.

00:15:39.071 --> 00:15:42.875
With token level interactions, you can expect such multi-token sequences

00:15:42.875 --> 00:15:45.778
to be better matched between the documents and the queries.

00:15:46.211 --> 00:15:50.716
It's time to run search using both approaches to retrieve the results.

00:15:51.116 --> 00:15:52.918
Here you can see how long

00:15:52.918 --> 00:15:55.020
it took to generate ColBERT Dense vector,

00:15:55.321 --> 00:15:58.223
and also how long it takes to return the results.

00:15:58.791 --> 00:16:01.293
We have another helper function

00:16:01.393 --> 00:16:05.597
with a simple visual comparing the search results of both methods.

00:16:06.298 --> 00:16:09.802
One key advantage of ColBERT's token level matching

00:16:09.902 --> 00:16:13.205
is its ability to capture specific terms like proper names.

00:16:13.505 --> 00:16:17.676
In this case, Qdrant, better than pulled dense vectors,

00:16:17.776 --> 00:16:23.082
since individual tokens retain their semantic identity throughout the matching process.

00:16:23.082 --> 00:16:26.952
Notice there are differences between ColBERT and dense embedding results,

00:16:27.186 --> 00:16:29.455
but it's hard to say what was the winner.

00:16:29.955 --> 00:16:32.925
There is an overlap, but eyeballing is usually not enough.

00:16:33.192 --> 00:16:36.562
In real-world cases, we build reference ground truth datasets

00:16:36.729 --> 00:16:39.164
to ensure the returned results are of a high quality.

00:16:39.565 --> 00:16:42.368
What I'm noticing from this simple example

00:16:42.368 --> 00:16:44.770
is that all of the ColBERT results mention Qdrant,

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:47.272
which is not true for the dense results.

00:16:47.473 --> 00:16:51.176
Which is kind of expected because our query has mentioned Qdrant.

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:54.713
Let's check how each of the query tokens contributed to the score

00:16:54.713 --> 00:16:57.316
for the best result as retrieved by ColBERT.

00:16:57.516 --> 00:17:00.319
We can use the same heat map like you used before.

00:17:00.819 --> 00:17:04.023
For that, we need the tokenization of both the document and query

00:17:04.023 --> 00:17:06.325
as well as their ColBERT representations.

00:17:06.592 --> 00:17:09.194
A similarity matrix is the last missing piece.

00:17:09.595 --> 00:17:13.399
As all the components are ready, let's finally display the heat map.

00:17:14.199 --> 00:17:18.203
It's not surprising that perform and performance were the closest neighbors.

00:17:18.404 --> 00:17:20.372
However, Q

00:17:20.372 --> 00:17:23.342
dran, and t were also contributing

00:17:23.375 --> 00:17:26.145
to the final MaxSim scores quite significantly.

00:17:26.545 --> 00:17:30.049
So, even though ColBERT does not understand what Qdrant means,

00:17:30.249 --> 00:17:32.851
this token level interactions helped to implement

00:17:32.885 --> 00:17:34.553
keyword matching.

00:17:34.953 --> 00:17:37.056
That was just a very quick introduction

00:17:37.056 --> 00:17:39.858
to how multi-vector techniques work for text.

00:17:40.259 --> 00:17:42.561
Let's join me in the next lesson to see what

00:17:42.561 --> 00:17:44.663
those techniques look like for images.
